/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Gasoline (petrol) is a blend of a few hundred hydrocarbons, mostly with 4 to 12 carbon atoms: paraffins, iso-paraffins, olefins, naphthenes and aromatics.
   - Volatility is the readiness to evaporate.
   - Octane number is the percentage by volume of iso-octane (rated 100) in a blend with n-heptane (rated 0) that knocks like the fuel in a standard test engine.
   - An SI engine runs near λ = 1 for most of its life so that the three-way catalyst works.
   - Ethanol carries oxygen in its molecule, so it needs less air (stoichiometric air–fuel ratio 9.0) and has less energy per kg (about 26.8 MJ/kg).
   - At a steady speed, throttle and spark, consecutive traces still do not lie on top of each other.
   - Causes.
   - Effects.
   - Measuring it.
   - Reducing it.
*/
