/* book Worked example: Q: (a) Find the stoichiometric air–fuel ratio of iso-octane, C8H18, taking air as 21 % O2 and 79 % N2 by volume (molar mass of air 28.97). (b) E20 petrol is 20 % ethanol by volume. Take the densities of ethanol and petrol as 789 and 745 kg/m³, stoichiometric air–fuel ratios 9.0 and 14.7, and calorific values 26.8 and 44 MJ/kg. Find the blend's stoichiometric air–fuel ratio and calorific value, and compare the energy per kg of stoichiometric mixture.
   1. Balance: C8H18 + a(O2 + 3.76 N2) → 8 CO2 + 9 H2O + 3.76a N2. Oxygen: 2a = 16 + 9, so a = 12.5.
   2. Air per kmol of fuel: 12.5 × 4.76 = 59.5 kmol, that is 59.5 × 28.97 = 1723.7 kg.
   3. Fuel: 8 × 12.011 + 18 × 1.008 = 114.23 kg. Air–fuel ratio = 1723.7 ÷ 114.23 = 15.09.
   4. Mass fraction of ethanol in E20: (0.2 × 789) ÷ (0.2 × 789 + 0.8 × 745) = 157.8 ÷ 753.8 = 0.209.
   5. Blend air–fuel ratio (by mass): 0.209 × 9.0 + 0.791 × 14.7 = 1.88 + 11.63 = 13.5.
   6. Blend calorific value: 0.209 × 26.8 + 0.791 × 44 = 5.61 + 34.79 = 40.4 MJ/kg, about 8 % less than petrol.
   7. Energy per kg of stoichiometric mixture: petrol 44 ÷ (14.7 + 1) = 2.80 MJ/kg; E20 40.4 ÷ (13.5 + 1) = 2.79 MJ/kg.
   Answer: (a) 15.1 : 1. (b) E20 needs 13.5 : 1 and gives 40.4 MJ/kg, yet a cylinder full of stoichiometric E20 mixture carries almost the same energy as petrol (2.79 against 2.80 MJ/kg); the engine simply injects about 8 % more fuel by mass. */
