/* book exam traps: In knock the spark flame still burns most of the charge; only the end gas autoignites. Do not write that knock is "burning that does not start at the plug". | Knock starts after the spark; pre-ignition starts before it. Compare the event with the spark angle, not with TDC. | Retarding the spark reduces knock but does not stop pre-ignition, which is lit by a hot spot. | Knock is not only a petrol-engine problem: CI engines also knock, from a long ignition delay at the start of combustion (see diesel knock). | If a question asks for the shock-driven, most severe form of knock, answer detonation; elsewhere the two words are used for the same thing. */
